  "notes": [
    "Generic capital and funding terms over-match credit, bank capital, and macro stories.",
    "Phrase-level offering terms are safer than a standalone offering match.",
    "Bare REIT matches every REIT earnings and share-price story; qualify it as non-traded REIT or REIT offering so only the offering side lands here.",
    "Direct participation, non-traded REIT/BDC, DST, and interval-fund terms belong here rather than in Investment Products, because the product is defined by how it is offered.",
    "Rule 506(b) and 506(c) are listed separately from Rule 506: the general-solicitation split is the distinction practitioners actually write about.",
    "Reg A and Reg CF ongoing-reporting form types (1-K, 1-SA, 1-U, C-AR, C-U) are deliberately absent. They appear in EDGAR form metadata, effectively never in prose, so keyword matching cannot reach them; they belong to filing ingestion instead."
  ],
